  "pd": {
    "basis": "published-pre-1930",
    "detail": "Traditional Scottish air, in circulation well before its first US publication in 1881, when Charles E. Pratt issued it (under the pseudonyms J. T. Wood and H. J. Fulmer) as 'Bring Back My Bonnie to Me'.",
    "sourceEdition": "Pratt (as J. T. Wood / H. J. Fulmer), 'Bring Back My Bonnie to Me' (1881); standard modern songbook notation in 3/4",
    "notes": "Encoded in its real meter, 3/4, quarter-note pulse (3 beats per bar). Melody and changes only; no lyrics. The song starts on a pickup ('My' = deg 5 oct -1): it lives at the end of the intro, at the ends of verse bars 4, 8 and 12 ('My'/'My'/'Oh'), and at the end of chorus bar 16 so a following verse gets its upbeat. The 'sea' phrase (verse bars 6-8) descends 1 7 6 below the tonic and half-cadences held on the leading tone (deg 7 oct -1) over D7."
  },
